// An LRU cache — the canonical `weak var` site, probed to find out whether
// `examples/Interpreter`'s verdict generalises.
//
// It does not, and the reason is interesting: this is the textbook reference-
// cycle data structure, and in elp it needs no handles, no cells and no
// `Weak<T>`. The thing that dissolves it is a collection decision made for
// unrelated reasons.

import { Entry } from elp.collections

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// GAP 1 — the version every textbook writes, and it is unconstructible here.
// Fifth sighting of one wall.
//
// An LRU cache is `HashMap<K, *Node>` plus a doubly-linked list, because the
// list gives O(1) move-to-front on a hit and O(1) eviction from the tail:
//
//   export object Node<K, V> {
//       const key   K,
//       var   value V,
//       var   prev  Optional<Confined<Node<K, V>>>,
//       var   next  Optional<Confined<Node<K, V>>>,
//   }
//
// In Swift this is the canonical `weak var prev`. In Rust it is
// `Weak<RefCell<Node>>` and it is the example everyone cites to explain why
// `Weak` exists.
//
// It cannot be written. `Node` holds `Confined<Node>`, so `Node` is `Move`
// field-wise, and a cell holding a `Move` aggregate cannot have its fields
// updated — so `node.next = other` has no spelling. Same wall as
// `examples/Recalc` GAP 1, `examples/Playhead` GAP 6, `tree-places.elp` and
// `examples/Interpreter` FINDING 7.
//
// Unlike all four of those, **nothing is lost by it being blocked**, because
// the structure is not needed.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// FINDING 1 — elp's `Map` is insertion-ordered, which makes it a strictly
// stronger primitive than a hash map, and the entire linked list evaporates.
//
// `collections.md` makes `Map` insertion-ordered. That decision was made for
// iteration predictability, and it happens to supply exactly the recency order
// an LRU cache maintains a second data structure for: remove a key and
// re-insert it, and it moves to the end of the order.
//
// So the cache is one `Map`, ownership is a tree, every refcount is one, and
// there is no cycle to leak. The canonical `Weak` site needs no `Weak`.
//
// Worth being precise about what this does and does not show. It is **not** a
// counterexample to `examples/Interpreter` — that cycle is in the semantics of
// `let rec` and no data structure choice reaches it. What it shows is that the
// two famous cycle shapes have different causes: a *data structure's*
// back-edge is usually an index in disguise and dissolves under a better
// primitive, while a *language's* cyclic value does not. `Weak<T>` is wanted
// for the second, and the first is where it is usually taught.
export object LruCache<K, V> {
	const capacity int,
	var entries Map<K, V>,
}

LruCache implements Move

export fn LruCache<K, V>.withCapacity(capacity int) -> Self {
	return Self { capacity, entries = .new() }
}

// A hit has to re-insert to refresh recency, which is the whole trick.
export fn LruCache<K, V>.get(&var self, key K) -> Optional<V> {
	// GAP 2 — `Map.get` on a `Move` value, sixth sighting, and here it is
	// benign in a way worth recording: a cache's `V` is whatever the caller
	// stored, so this is the one site where the three candidates
	// (`Duplicate`, move-out, error) are all *defensible* and the choice is a
	// real design decision rather than a forced one.
	//
	// A move-out is arguably right here — `remove` then `set` is literally
	// what this function does — which is the first evidence in the corpus for
	// the move-out candidate. `examples/Interpreter` FINDING 5 is evidence
	// against it for the same operation on an environment. So `Map.get` wants
	// two spellings, and that is a cleaner finding than the open question has
	// had so far.
	const found = self.entries.remove(key) ?? return nil

	self.entries.set(key, found)

	return .SOME(found)
}

export fn LruCache<K, V>.put(&var self, key K, value V) {
	// Re-inserting an existing key must refresh its position, so remove first
	// unconditionally rather than letting `set` overwrite in place. Whether
	// `Map.set` on an existing key preserves the original insertion position
	// or moves it to the end is **not stated anywhere**, and this cache is
	// correct under one reading and silently wrong under the other.
	//
	// FINDING 2 — that is a genuine hole in `collections.md`, found by the
	// first program whose correctness depends on it. "Insertion-ordered" names
	// the order for *new* keys and says nothing about re-insertion, and both
	// answers are defensible: preserving position matches "the order keys were
	// first seen", moving to the end matches "the order keys were last
	// written". An LRU cache needs the second; a stable config map wants the
	// first.
	self.entries.remove(key)
	self.entries.set(key, value)

	if self.entries.count() > self.capacity {
		self.evict()
	}
}

// FINDING 3, RESOLVED — and this file is the evidence that closed it.
//
// Eviction needs "the oldest entry", which is the first in iteration order
// and therefore one step of the iterator. With no `break`, a `for` that
// stops after the first element could not be written, and the only access to
// order was the protocol itself — so the original spelling materialised every
// key to use one, turning the single operation an LRU cache exists to make
// cheap into a linear scan.
//
// `control-flow.md` adds `break`, citing this finding, and the fix is the
// four lines below. Worth noting the alternative that was available and was
// not taken: a `Map.first()` would have fixed this one site, and `break`
// generalises to every other early exit in the language.
fn LruCache<K, V>.evict(&var self) {
	for key in self.entries.keys() {
		self.entries.remove(key)
		break
	}
}

export fn LruCache<K, V>.count(&self) -> int {
	return self.entries.count()
}

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// VERDICT — `Weak<T>` is still mandatory, but not for this.
//
// Three probes have now asked the cycle question and the answers are
// consistent once the shapes are separated:
//
//   | shape | cycle? | why |
//   |---|---|---|
//   | a dependency graph (`examples/Recalc`) | no | edges are addresses; ownership is a tree and the arena's lifetime is the program's |
//   | an LRU cache (here) | no | the back-edge is recency order, and an insertion-ordered `Map` already is one |
//   | `let rec` (`examples/Interpreter`) | **yes** | the cycle is in the semantics; no encoding reaches it, and the arena escape becomes a hand-written GC |
//
// The pattern: a **data structure's** back-edge is an index in disguise and
// dissolves under a better primitive. A **language's** cyclic value does not.
// `Weak<T>` is taught with the first and needed for the second.
//
// Which also means the `Weak<T>` design has a narrower target than the prior
// art suggests. It does not have to be ergonomic enough for everyday graph
// code, because everyday graph code should not use it. That matters for the
// open cost question — `aliasing.md`'s clash between `weak`'s zeroing at
// deallocation and `@abi(.C, realtime)` forbidding a decrement to zero — since
// a rarely-used `Weak<T>` can afford to be expensive, and could even be
// forbidden outright in a realtime body rather than made cheap.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// vim: ft=kotlin
